  "Question": "A boy buys oranges at \\(3\\) for \\(10\\) cents. He will sell them at \\(5\\) for \\(20\\) cents. In order to make a profit of \\(\\$1.00,\\) he must sell:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(67\\) oranges",
    "B": "\\(150\\) oranges",
    "C": "\\(200\\) oranges",
    "D": "an infinite number of oranges",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Use a batch of \\(15\\) oranges so that both groups of \\(3\\) and groups of \\(5\\) divide evenly. Buying \\(15\\) oranges costs\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{3}\\cdot10=50\\text{ cents},\n\\]\nwhile selling them brings in\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{5}\\cdot20=60\\text{ cents}.\n\\]\nThus each batch earns \\(60-50=10\\) cents. A profit of \\(\\$1.00=100\\) cents requires \\(100/10=10\\) batches, containing \\(10\\cdot15=150\\) oranges, choice B.",
